  "configuration_id": "config_009",
  "reason": "Evidence: The best configuration so far (config_050: pca_n2000, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, weighted, multiprofile) achieves 0.6352 acc / 0.6183 F1. All completed experiments used pca_n2000 as the RNA encoder, yet RNA is the dominant modality (RNA-only 0.485 vs image-only 0.227), making the RNA representation choice a high-impact, completely unexplored dimension. Question: Does replacing the linear PCA representation with a pretrained nonlinear encoder (scgpt) improve multimodal performance, while holding all other settings (multiprofile, imagenet_vit_l, avgpool, weighted) at their best-known values? Why this experiment: It changes exactly one variable (RNA encoder) from the current best config, cleanly isolating the representation effect. scgpt is the most expressive RNA encoder in the pool; if it fails to beat PCA, that is strong evidence that the linear representation is already sufficient and further gains must come from other axes (image encoder, aggregation). This maximizes information per experiment under the limited budget."
}

